 </section>

 <h2 id="check">Check your understanding</h2>
 <p>One or more questions for each outcome at the top of the page; the quiz in <a href="#what">What SEAD and DEAD are</a> and Figures 2 and 3 cover more.</p>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 1</span> A battery stays silent all through your strike because its crew fears an ARM. Has it been suppressed?</summary><p>Yes. SEAD includes neutralizing or temporarily degrading a defence, and keeping operators from turning their radars on was the 1991 plan.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 1</span> A planned strike target's SAM moves before the attack and you engage it where you find it. Which category is that now?</summary><p>Opportune suppression: any movement from targeted locations turns localized suppression into opportune suppression.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 2</span> Name four kinds of IADS target besides SAM sites.</summary><p>Any four of: command and control nodes, anti-aircraft artillery, early-warning, fire-control and GCI radars, SAM carriers and storage bunkers, and air defence operators and maintainers.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 3</span> Why does flying low hide you from a distant radar, and what does it cost?</summary><p>The earth's curve puts you below its radar horizon, and terrain can mask you; but low altitude is where guns and short-range missiles are most dangerous.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 4</span> Why carry decoys if you already have anti-radiation missiles?</summary><p>An ARM finds its target by the radar's transmissions. Decoys make radars come on to track them, take shots meant for manned aircraft, and so give the ARMs their targets.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 5</span> With the same jammer, why are strikers covered by a stand-off jammer seen sooner than if they carried it themselves?</summary><p>Their echo grows as they close on the radar, but the jamming does not, because the jammer stays far away; so the echo overcomes it at a longer range. In return the stand-off jammer is safe from home-on-jam weapons.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 6</span> Your HARM display shows an unknown emitter near the friendly lines. What does joint doctrine warn about?</summary><p>Friendly emitters not identified to SEAD crews can look unknown or hostile and have been attacked; the rules of engagement require correct identification before firing at unknown or spurious signals, though self-defense is always allowed.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 6</span> Who should hear about a SAM site you saw but could not attack?</summary><p>The agency controlling your mission, which passes it on to whoever can target it.</p></details>

 <h2 id="sources">Sources and applicability</h2>
 <h3>Military sources</h3>
 <p id="source-jp"><strong>[1] US Joint Chiefs of Staff, JP 3-01, Countering Air and Missile Threats, 23 March 2012.</strong> <a href="https://www.bits.de/NRANEU/others/jp-doctrine/jp3_01(12).pdf">Read on the Berlin Information-center for Transatlantic Security's copy</a>, 3 November 2026, by printed page: JRFL, p. III-24; offensive counterair and the Gulf War SEAD plan, p. IV-4; SEAD, pp. IV-12 to IV-16; fighter escort, pp. IV-16 to IV-17; layered defence, p. V-2; weapon engagement zones, pp. V-10 to V-11; identification, p. A-2; glossary, pp. GL-8 to GL-18. The joint publication for all four services. A later edition exists on the Joint Chiefs' own site, which could not be read from the authoring environment, so some wording may have changed. It refers to a multi-service SEAD tactics publication, which was not available.</p>
 <p id="source-usna"><strong>[2] US Naval Academy, Weapons and Systems Engineering Department, Fundamentals of Naval Weapons Systems, chapter 11, Countermeasures.</strong> <a href="https://man.fas.org/dod-101/navy/docs/fun/part11.htm">Read on the Federation of American Scientists' copy</a>, 3 November 2026, by section: 11.2.2 to 11.2.2.12 and 11.2.3.4. A textbook for midshipmen; its principles apply to any service.</p>
 <h3>Secondary sources</h3>
 <p id="source-wiki"><strong>[3] Wikipedia, "Suppression of Enemy Air Defenses".</strong> <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Suppression_of_Enemy_Air_Defenses">Read 3 November 2026.</a> Used for the DEAD distinction, history and weapons.</p>
 <p id="source-rh"><strong>[4] Wikipedia, "Radar horizon".</strong> <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Radar_horizon">Read 3 November 2026.</a> Used for the 4/3 effective earth radius and its examples.</p>
 <h3>Sim sources</h3>
 <p id="source-f16"><strong>[5] Eagle Dynamics, DCS: F-16C Viper Early Access Guide (English).</strong> <a href="https://www.digitalcombatsimulator.com/en/downloads/documentation/">DCS documentation page</a>, read 3 November 2026, pp. 525, 657 and 665–668; p. 534 read 5 November 2026. A sim manual, cited only for the In the sim panel.</p>
 <p id="source-bms"><strong>[6] Benchmark Sims, Falcon BMS 4.38.1 Training Manual.</strong> <a href="https://cdn.falcon-bms.com/docs/4.38/BMS-Training-Manual.pdf">PDF</a>, read 3 November 2026, pp. 210 and 372–381; pp. 195–197 read 5 November 2026. A sim manual, cited only for the In the sim panel.</p>
 <p>Page-specific excerpts are recorded in the repository review evidence: <a href="https://github.com/burnzoire/oversweep/blob/main/docs/reviews/sead-dead-evidence.md">SEAD and DEAD evidence</a>.</p>
 <p>Figures 1 to 3 and the worked package are original, invented examples, not reproductions of any publication's figure.</p>
 <p class="note">Applicability: written for aircrew of every service who fly with or against surface air defences. Doctrine is from the joint publication, which all four services follow; no service's own SEAD tactics publication could be read, so no service-specific tactics are included. Theater rules of engagement, SPINS, your unit's procedures and the current editions govern. This revision is Drafted pending independent source-backed review.</p>
